1
00:00:05,682 --> 00:00:10,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint is the the practice of non-distraction.

2
00:00:11,052 --> 00:00:11,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes.

3
00:00:14,452 --> 00:00:21,195
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you... how can you go about this, like, not being... not distracting yourself all the time?

4
00:00:22,296 --> 00:00:26,598
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way that... it says... the Buddha says, he's an intent on wakefulness.

5
00:00:27,278 --> 00:00:28,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

6
00:00:28,099 --> 00:00:28,559
[SPEAKER_00]: Intent on...

7
00:00:28,859 --> 00:00:29,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

8
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:30,020
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly that.

9
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:33,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not, like, holding on to your wakefulness neurotically.

10
00:00:33,761 --> 00:00:35,622
[SPEAKER_00]: You're basically intent on not losing it.

11
00:00:35,702 --> 00:00:36,423
[SPEAKER_00]: And how you lose it?

12
00:00:36,503 --> 00:00:38,904
[SPEAKER_00]: You lose it by giving in to a non-wakeful intention.

13
00:00:39,845 --> 00:00:42,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you cannot accidentally lose your wakefulness.

14
00:00:43,567 --> 00:00:50,430
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even lapsing in terms of your thoughts or something similar, it's not necessarily you lost wakefulness.

15
00:00:51,310 --> 00:00:57,313
[SPEAKER_00]: You lose wakefulness when you intentionally give in to distraction or lust or anger and aversion.

16
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:07,822
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, in relation to the previous... well, one of the previous talks was to keep questioning yourself, your motivation, so that your own intentions can become transparent and clear.

17
00:01:08,303 --> 00:01:15,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you will know, even if sometimes people say, oh, you know, I'm engaged with, like, spreading the dhamma, but they're doing it.

18
00:01:15,048 --> 00:01:18,710
[SPEAKER_00]: Their intentions are rooted in the desire for distraction.

19
00:01:19,531 --> 00:01:26,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire for saying something to do, giving this this worldly purpose to my life, because otherwise, just boredom and anxiety sets in and it's too much to handle.

20
00:01:27,575 --> 00:01:28,956
[SPEAKER_00]: And in that case, that's not the dhamma.

21
00:01:29,757 --> 00:01:40,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Although you might be actually parroting the Buddha's words and publishing the Buddha's words, it's not the actual dhamma, which is always non-aversion, non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion, non-distraction, wakefulness and knowledge.

22
00:01:41,563 --> 00:01:42,843
They won't think, you know, not...

23
00:01:48,921 --> 00:01:50,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Like being able to just relax.

24
00:01:51,763 --> 00:01:52,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

25
00:01:52,103 --> 00:01:53,724
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you can relax perfectly.

26
00:01:53,864 --> 00:02:02,709
[SPEAKER_00]: You can actually... You would be relaxed all the time, if you can start discerning your intentions and then just... You don't have to... And that's the problem.

27
00:02:02,729 --> 00:02:08,793
[SPEAKER_00]: When people don't discern intentions, they tend to either indulge them all or dismiss them all, because they don't see anything in between.

28
00:02:11,905 --> 00:02:19,497
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person starts discerning their own motivation, their own intentions, then you know, actually, these intentions are not rooted in greed, nor aversion, nor delusion.

29
00:02:19,658 --> 00:02:19,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Fine.

30
00:02:20,158 --> 00:02:20,639
[SPEAKER_00]: No problem.

31
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:22,122
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you get relaxed.

32
00:02:23,143 --> 00:02:29,585
[SPEAKER_00]: So, only you have to abandon those that are rooted in desire for distraction, desire for sensuality, desire for aversion, and so on.

33
00:02:29,845 --> 00:02:40,147
[SPEAKER_01]: I suppose, when you're coming from, you know, just relaxing into greed, or what you say, relaxing into delusion, that's all you know.

34
00:02:40,167 --> 00:02:43,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know this other way of relaxing.

35
00:02:47,390 --> 00:02:53,053
[SPEAKER_00]: Initially, the only notion of relaxation is relaxation of distraction that you have.

36
00:02:53,733 --> 00:02:58,676
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's, yeah, that type of relaxation needs to be abandoned, because it's not relaxation, really.

37
00:03:03,058 --> 00:03:09,082
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can call it relaxation, but it's not peace, because you're fully controlled by it and fully dependent upon it.

38
00:03:11,504 --> 00:03:13,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Like watching a movie.

39
00:03:13,445 --> 00:03:13,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

40
00:03:13,665 --> 00:03:17,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's all the sounds, sights, blasts, bang-bang.

41
00:03:17,207 --> 00:03:17,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

42
00:03:18,368 --> 00:03:18,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Dramatic.

43
00:03:18,968 --> 00:03:19,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it is.

44
00:03:20,089 --> 00:03:21,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, you're out through your senses.

45
00:03:21,789 --> 00:03:33,876
[SPEAKER_00]: You might not necessarily be actively pursuing the sensual, the full-on sensual pleasures, but it's still bound with the sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, which means it's still within the sensual domain.

46
00:03:34,853 --> 00:03:38,274
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's not just you have to abandon the obvious course, sensuality.

47
00:03:38,494 --> 00:03:42,416
[SPEAKER_00]: You also have to abandon distraction that you've been gaining through your senses.

48
00:03:42,436 --> 00:03:46,697
[SPEAKER_00]: But when I say abandon, it doesn't mean you obliterate any notion of your senses and deny it.

49
00:03:47,177 --> 00:04:01,922
[SPEAKER_00]: I just mean make sure that if you are engaging, that motivation, the intentions behind that engagement is not rooted in greed, aversion, delusion, which is basically delusion equals wanting distraction.

50
00:04:03,863 --> 00:04:04,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-waiting.

51
00:04:05,545 --> 00:04:08,587
[SPEAKER_00]: All of those would contribute to non-wakefulness.

52
00:04:09,508 --> 00:04:10,048
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's it.

53
00:04:10,229 --> 00:04:13,050
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, automatically, if it's not those, then it's automatically wakefulness.

54
00:04:13,911 --> 00:04:16,313
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you don't... you're not doing the wakefulness.

55
00:04:17,253 --> 00:04:22,437
[SPEAKER_00]: You're developing it, basically, by preventing non-wakefulness to overwhelm you.

56
00:04:22,617 --> 00:04:24,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, intentionally, to give in to it.

57
00:04:24,959 --> 00:04:27,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you prevent that, and then you're automatically wakeful.

58
00:04:30,082 --> 00:04:31,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it becomes effortless.

59
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:33,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, because you're not doing it.

60
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,461
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a method or something to uphold constantly every second of your life.

61
00:04:36,481 --> 00:04:38,502
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how you get truly relaxed, truly peaceful.

62
00:04:40,122 --> 00:04:40,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's effortless.

63
00:04:41,803 --> 00:04:48,125
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes effort to give in to intentions, to do things upon them, be it central or non-central.

64
00:04:49,005 --> 00:04:58,228
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you, all you do is just abstain from the effort of engaging with sensuality, aversion, distraction, then your experience is automatically effortless and wakeful.

65
00:05:05,716 --> 00:05:09,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, within that, well, you'll know exactly what to do, if you get to that point.

